  "year": 2025,
  "arxivId": "2410.02536",
  "abstract": "This paper explores how the complexity of rule-based systems influences the emergence of intelligent behavior in artificial systems. By training large language models on data generated by elementary cellular automata, the authors demonstrate that models trained on more complex data exhibit greater predictive ability and perform better on downstream tasks, revealing a 'sweet spot' of complexity conducive to intelligence.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The emergence of intelligent behavior in artificial systems can be studied by training models on simple systems that generate complex behaviors. <strong>Elementary cellular automata</strong> (ECAs) are one-dimensional systems that can produce a wide range of patterns, from simple to highly complex. By training large language models on data generated by ECAs, we can investigate how the complexity of the data influences the model's ability to learn effective representations.",
